From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 05:06:53 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 4/4] fix(#20): a test fingerprints the same whichever route
 collected it, so a written test's trace row is reused (Detective #100)
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Defect, verbatim (Detective #100): "A written test still fingerprints differently in the next session; the
cause is not found." Found in Detective 644c3ac for its verdict cache, and Wesker's trace_cache.test_fingerprint
has the same split: a test written after a session collected is re-collected as a bare name (test_x) in one
session and a full node id (tests/t.py::test_x) in the next, and a live item stamps pytest's own
_pytest/python.py as a fixture file while a re-collected one stamps none, so the same test hashed two ways and
the row the writing session stored was never found by the next.

The obvious fix (strip the id to its last segment) is wrong: it would merge two same-named tests in different
files, and a parametrized case must keep its case. The identity is the test's FILE (real path) and the member
path after the first "::", parametrization included.

Pure decisions (pinned):
  canonical_test_identity(node_id, origin)  "<origin>::<member>" for a full id and for a bare name alike;
    the id it has when the file is unknown.
  fixture_origin_scope(path, installed_roots)  installed (under the standard library or site-packages: the
    regime digest already binds those versions, and only a live item stamps them) / project (stays in the
    hash, so editing a conftest or fixture file still invalidates the row).

Not closed: the two pure functions are the same rule as Detective.verdict_cache's and are duplicated, not
shared (Detective cannot import this change until its lock pins it; the integrator may point Detective at
these). Existing trace-cache rows miss once under the new key. Pinning canonical_test_identity with converge
is refused: tests/test_pytest_discovery.py::test_parametrized_cases_fingerprint_apart reaches it and fails in
the isolated session on the base too (reproduced on trace_cache._sha without this change).

Receipt: fixture_origin_scope ✓ COMPLETE (operator universe · modulo 1 crash-only value gap) · 14/14 killed
tests/test_trace_fingerprint_spelling_intent.py 23 passed (3.14 and 3.12); neighbouring trace/guard files 116
passed on both; with the old spelling and fixture rule restored 2 of 23 fail (the live-vs-re-collected
fingerprints). ruff clean under pinned 0.16.7
